1
00:00:01,500 --> 00:00:05,374
Lesson 3.3: Formulating the Care Plan's Problem Note.

2
00:00:05,414 --> 00:00:08,977
Part 1: Condition and Its Medication.

3
00:00:10,633 --> 00:00:13,762
This part covers the first two lines of the problem note:

4
00:00:13,802 --> 00:00:17,497
the condition, and the medications that manage it.

5
00:00:17,547 --> 00:00:20,058
Three documentation standards apply.

6
00:00:20,108 --> 00:00:23,249
First, the medication name includes its concentration.

7
00:00:23,289 --> 00:00:26,735
If it's missing, add it from the directions.

8
00:00:26,785 --> 00:00:30,261
Second, a PRN medication, given only as needed,

9
00:00:30,301 --> 00:00:36,063
can't be the only CCM medication. A scheduled one is required.

10
00:00:36,113 --> 00:00:41,762
Third, judge which medications treat the condition with your medical knowledge and the Reference tab,

11
00:00:41,802 --> 00:00:43,901
not the directions alone.

12
00:00:45,167 --> 00:00:50,293
For this scratch patient, you've located the profile, and confirmed eligibility.

13
00:00:50,343 --> 00:00:52,488
Next, you formulate the problem note:

14
00:00:52,528 --> 00:00:56,357
the care plan template for the chronic condition you monitor.

15
00:00:56,407 --> 00:00:57,823
It starts with the condition,

16
00:00:57,863 --> 00:01:01,849
the medications that manage it, and medication adherence.

17
00:01:01,900 --> 00:01:04,927
Then come trends in labs and vitals, gaps in care,

18
00:01:04,967 --> 00:01:08,667
risk factors and complications, and symptoms.

19
00:01:09,667 --> 00:01:13,731
Further down: falls, provider visits, hospital and ER visits,

20
00:01:13,771 --> 00:01:17,455
referrals, medication changes, and escalation.

21
00:01:17,900 --> 00:01:20,209
In this part, you fill in the first two lines:

22
00:01:20,249 --> 00:01:23,940
the condition, and the medications that manage it.

23
00:01:28,633 --> 00:01:29,806
Start with the condition.

24
00:01:29,846 --> 00:01:36,580
The task information gives its ICD-10 code. Here, it's E78.5.

25
00:01:38,233 --> 00:01:42,589
At the top of the patient's record, the banner shows general information,

26
00:01:46,700 --> 00:01:48,701
such as the patient's allergies.

27
00:01:50,367 --> 00:01:53,574
The most recent vitals are in the top right corner.

28
00:01:53,833 --> 00:01:57,316
Below the banner, the record is divided into tabs.

29
00:01:58,267 --> 00:02:00,626
Each tab holds different information.

30
00:02:00,686 --> 00:02:04,279
Open MED DIAG, short for medical diagnosis.

31
00:02:06,467 --> 00:02:10,446
It lists the patient's active diagnoses, chronic or acute.

32
00:02:11,411 --> 00:02:15,181
Search the page for the code: E78.5.

33
00:02:16,833 --> 00:02:20,754
E78.5 is hyperlipidemia, unspecified.

34
00:02:20,794 --> 00:02:25,423
So the condition you follow up on is hyperlipidemia.

35
00:02:25,473 --> 00:02:30,533
On the note's first line, replace the code with the condition: hyperlipidemia.

36
00:02:34,090 --> 00:02:37,268
Next, the medications. Open the Orders tab.

37
00:02:38,933 --> 00:02:42,784
It lists all of the patient's orders, not only medications.

38
00:02:43,055 --> 00:02:45,933
To filter them, open Display Filters.

39
00:02:46,867 --> 00:02:50,445
Order Category lists the kinds of orders: pharmacy, diet,

40
00:02:50,485 --> 00:02:55,235
supplement, laboratory, diagnostic, other, and enteral feed.

41
00:02:55,300 --> 00:03:00,049
You only need medications. So keep Pharmacy checked, and clear the rest.

42
00:03:01,742 --> 00:03:03,147
Then click Search.

43
00:03:04,433 --> 00:03:07,573
Now the list shows only the patient's medications.

44
00:03:09,233 --> 00:03:10,660
They're all active orders:

45
00:03:10,700 --> 00:03:14,719
the patient takes every one of them now, whatever the start date.

46
00:03:15,667 --> 00:03:22,314
To manage hyperlipidemia, the patient takes Rosuvastatin Calcium Tablet 20 MG.

47
00:03:22,567 --> 00:03:27,731
Its directions: give 1 tablet by mouth at bedtime, for lipid control.

48
00:03:28,000 --> 00:03:33,184
In the note, the second line reads: continues to be managed through medications.

49
00:03:35,400 --> 00:03:39,846
Copy the medication name exactly as it's written in PointClickCare,

50
00:03:42,133 --> 00:03:43,405
and paste it on that line:

51
00:03:43,445 --> 00:03:49,480
continues to be managed through Rosuvastatin Calcium Tablet 20 MG.

52
00:03:50,533 --> 00:03:55,878
The name you document must include its concentration: here, 20 MG.

53
00:03:55,928 --> 00:04:00,546
Some order names leave it out, like Rosuvastatin Calcium Tablet.

54
00:04:00,596 --> 00:04:06,200
Then take the strength from the directions, for example, give 20 mg at bedtime,

55
00:04:06,250 --> 00:04:10,676
and add it: Rosuvastatin Calcium Tablet 20 MG.

56
00:04:12,333 --> 00:04:17,816
Standard 2 is about PRN orders: medications given only as needed.

57
00:04:17,867 --> 00:04:20,394
Their directions say as needed. For example,

58
00:04:20,434 --> 00:04:24,583
acetaminophen, every 8 hours as needed for pain,

59
00:04:24,633 --> 00:04:27,771
and albuterol, every 6 hours as needed.

60
00:04:27,833 --> 00:04:33,365
A PRN medication can't be the only CCM medication for a condition.

61
00:04:33,415 --> 00:04:38,205
The patient must also take a medication for that condition on a regular schedule.

62
00:04:38,245 --> 00:04:40,861
It's an eligibility requirement.

63
00:04:40,911 --> 00:04:45,067
For example, if the condition were COPD, Spiriva Respimat,

64
00:04:45,107 --> 00:04:49,115
taken every morning, would be that scheduled medication.

65
00:04:49,167 --> 00:04:52,819
Albuterol could be listed with it, but never on its own.

66
00:04:54,567 --> 00:04:59,204
Standard 3: don't rely only on the reason written in the directions.

67
00:04:59,267 --> 00:05:03,398
Rosuvastatin is a statin, so you know it treats hyperlipidemia,

68
00:05:03,438 --> 00:05:06,587
even if its directions didn't say so.

69
00:05:06,733 --> 00:05:11,720
To check your judgement, use the Reference tab in the CCM Clinical Tool.

70
00:05:12,667 --> 00:05:14,922
It has a reference for each condition.

71
00:05:17,100 --> 00:05:19,006
Select Hyperlipidemia.

72
00:05:21,200 --> 00:05:26,044
Medications by Line lists the first, second, and third line treatments.

73
00:05:27,267 --> 00:05:32,353
For hyperlipidemia, the first line is statins, such as rosuvastatin.

74
00:05:36,133 --> 00:05:40,125
The second line is ezetimibe, bempedoic acid, or both.

75
00:05:40,175 --> 00:05:45,030
And the third line, for very high risk, is PCSK9 inhibitors.

76
00:05:45,233 --> 00:05:48,945
So list every medication on this reference that the patient takes,

77
00:05:48,985 --> 00:05:53,004
not only those the directions mark for hyperlipidemia.

78
00:05:54,833 --> 00:05:59,841
The first two lines are done: the condition, and the medication that manages it.

79
00:06:01,233 --> 00:06:06,893
To recap: find the condition from the task's ICD-10 code, in MED DIAG.

80
00:06:06,943 --> 00:06:09,389
Filter the orders to pharmacy only.

81
00:06:09,439 --> 00:06:12,516
Copy the medication name exactly as written.

82
00:06:12,566 --> 00:06:17,612
Make sure it includes the concentration. If not, add it from the directions.

83
00:06:17,662 --> 00:06:22,708
Never list a PRN medication alone: a scheduled one is required.

84
00:06:22,758 --> 00:06:26,298
And judge with your medical knowledge and the Reference tab.

85
00:06:28,433 --> 00:06:29,714
Next, in Part 2,

86
00:06:29,754 --> 00:06:34,066
you check whether the patient takes these medications as prescribed.
